WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.075
Three more streams share the queue.

2
00:00:02.075 --> 00:00:07.413
Configuration first: a patched host that
drifts from its baseline isn't remediated.

3
00:00:07.413 --> 00:00:11.274
Baselines come from
SCuBA, CIS or DISA STIGs.

4
00:00:11.274 --> 00:00:16.410
Azure machine configuration's auto-correct
mode fixes drift without recording it,

5
00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:19.163
so the program keeps it
for low-risk settings.

6
00:00:19.163 --> 00:00:20.601
Cloud posture next.

7
00:00:20.601 --> 00:00:24.115
A storage account open to the
internet has no missing patch,

8
00:00:24.115 --> 00:00:25.837
so a scanner never reports it.

9
00:00:25.837 --> 00:00:29.837
Each provider's native posture tool
is primary for its own platform.

10
00:00:29.837 --> 00:00:32.969
Defender for Cloud doesn't
cover Oracle Cloud,

11
00:00:32.969 --> 00:00:36.925
and its AWS connector isn't
available in Azure Government.

12
00:00:36.925 --> 00:00:37.837
Then data.

13
00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:41.476
The CUI framework has two
levels, Basic and Specified,

14
00:00:41.476 --> 00:00:44.536
decided by the authority
in the CUI Registry:

15
00:00:44.536 --> 00:00:49.912
Privacy Act records are Basic, and
federal taxpayer information is Specified.

16
00:00:49.912 --> 00:00:54.975
Labels drive data loss prevention on
the service, the endpoint and the CASB.

17
00:00:54.975 --> 00:00:56.425
And check queue membership.

18
00:00:56.425 --> 00:01:01.039
Since early October 2026, a built-in
Microsoft tuning rule keeps DLP

19
00:01:01.039 --> 00:01:05.950
signals out of the incident queue
unless an administrator turns it off.
